.TH VIGCIPHER 1 2026-11-13 vigcipher "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vigcipher \- apply a Vigen\(`ere cipher to standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vigcipher
.BR \-e | \-d
.RB { \-a
.IR alphabet " | " \fB\-q\fP " " name }
.B \-k
.I key
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-u | \-l ]
.br
.B vigcipher
.BR \-h | \-v
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vigcipher
reads text from standard input, shifts each character through an
alphabet according to a repeating key, and writes the result to standard
output.
.PP
Each input character is looked up in the alphabet. It is moved forward
(when encrypting) or backward (when decrypting) by the position of the
current key character in the same alphabet, plus one, wrapping around at
the end. So with the alphabet
.B a\-z
the key character
.B a
shifts by 1 and
.B c
by 3. The next input character uses the next key character, and the key
repeats once it runs out.
.PP
The Vigen\(`ere cipher has been broken for well over a century.
.B vigcipher
is for fun and teaching only; do not use it to protect anything.
.SH OPTIONS
Exactly one of
.B \-e
or
.BR \-d ,
an alphabet
.RB ( \-a
or
.BR \-q ),
and a key
.RB ( \-k )
are required.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-alphabet =\fIalphabet\fP
The ordered set of characters that can be enciphered. Every character must
be unique. At most 256 characters.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-predefined\-alpha =\fIname\fP
Use a built-in alphabet instead of
.BR \-a .
.I name
is one of:
.RS
.TP 8
.B UC
.B A\-Z
.TP
.B LC
.B a\-z
.TP
.B AC
.B A\-Z
followed by
.B a\-z
.TP
.B PRINT
the printable ASCII characters from space to tilde (but see
.BR BUGS )
.RE
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key =\fIkey\fP
The characters that set how far each input character is shifted. Every
key character must be in the alphabet; repeats are allowed. At most 256
characters.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-encrypt
Shift characters forward through the alphabet.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-decrypt
Shift characters backward through the alphabet.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-passthru
Copy input characters that are not in the alphabet to the output
unchanged; the key does not advance for them. Without this option, such a
character is an error.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-upper
Convert the input, the alphabet and the key to upper case before
enciphering. This is an error if it leaves the alphabet with duplicate
characters.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-lower
Convert the input, the alphabet and the key to lower case before
enciphering. This is an error if it leaves the alphabet with duplicate
characters.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version number and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success;
.B 1
if an option is missing or invalid, or if the input contains a character
outside the alphabet and
.B \-p
was not given.
.SH EXAMPLES
Encrypt a message with the key
.BR lemon ,
keeping the spaces:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ printf 'attack at dawn' | vigcipher \-e \-p \-q LC \-k lemon
mygpqw fg sois
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decrypt it again:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ printf 'mygpqw fg sois' | vigcipher \-d \-p \-q LC \-k lemon
attack at dawn
.EE
.RE
.PP
A Caesar cipher is a one-character key. With
.B c
every letter moves three places:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ printf 'the' | vigcipher \-e \-q LC \-k c
wkh
.EE
.RE
.SH BUGS
The
.B PRINT
alphabet leaves out
.BR @ ,
so input containing
.B @
is rejected unless
.B \-p
is given.
.SH SEE ALSO
.UR https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vigen%C3%A8re_cipher
Vigen\(`ere cipher
.UE
